{"id": "sevt_01VKYArjvH3rt85x3GUEhh4h", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed. Multiple `order_items` queries per single `request_id` (the N+1 fan-out), first appearing at 14:08:57 \u2014 right after the deploy \u2014 and 152 pool-exhaustion events follow.\n\n## What happened\n\n- **p99 climb starts 14:08**, right after `07000f4` deployed (14:08:45) \u2014 not at 14:31. The 14:31 point is just where the already-saturated system crossed into multi-second territory.\n- `07000f4` replaced a single batched `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` query with a **per-order query inside a loop** (N+1). Each checkout summary now fires one DB query per open order instead of one total.\n- **db_pool_utilization** rises in lockstep from 14:08 and pins at **1.0 (fully saturated) by 14:39**; log shows 4,090 per-row queries and 152 pool-exhaustion events.\n- `510eca7` (14:26, rolled back 14:29:10) is a **red herring** \u2014 it only changed a log line from f-string to structured fields. It was rolled back and latency stayed high, which rules it out.\n\n> **Root cause:** `07000f4` \u2014 reintroduced an N+1 query (per-order `order_items` fetch in a loop instead of one batched `order_id__in` query), saturating the checkout DB pool and driving p99 from ~75ms to multi-second.\n\nNote: this commit is still live \u2014 rolling back `510eca7` did nothing because it wasn't the cause. `07000f4` needs to be reverted.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:49:21.018905Z", "type": "agent.message"}
